L000098	
L000099	\begin{abstract}
L000100	  We apply Continuous Normalizing Flows trained with the Flow Matching method
L000101	  to the problem of
L000102	  phase-space sampling in Monte Carlo event generation for high-energy collider physics.
L000103	  Focusing on lepton-pair and top-quark pair production with multiple jets, the two
L000104	  computationally most expensive processes at the Large Hadron Collider, we train
L000105	  helicity-conditioned Continuous Normalizing Flows to remap the random numbers used in matrix element evaluation.
L000106	  Compared to standard methods, we achieve unweighting efficiency improvements by factors of up to 184 and 25
L000107	  for the two processes at their respective highest jet number,
L000108	  at the cost of an increased evaluation time.
L000109	  When combining the advantages of Continuous Normalizing Flows
L000110	  with the fast evaluation times of Coupling Layer based Flows,
L000111	  using the RegFlow approach,
L000112	  we find parton-level unweighted event generation walltime gains
L000113	  of about a factor of ten at the highest jet numbers.
L000114	  These substantial gains
L000115	  highlight the promise of samplers based on machine learning for next-generation collider experiments.
L000116	\end{abstract}
L000117	
L000118	
L000119	\maketitle
L000120	\textit{Introduction}---The exceptional experimental
L000121	precision achieved by the ATLAS~\cite{ATLAS:2008xda} and CMS~\cite{CMS:2008xjf} experiments during the current and upcoming runs at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC),
L000122	and its potential successors~\cite{FCC:2018byv,FCC:2018evy,FCC:2018vvp}
L000123	demand equally precise
L000124	theoretical simulations.
L000125	Without any improvements over current techniques, the uncertainties in many measurements will be dominated
L000126	not by experimental statistics, but by deficiencies in Monte Carlo (MC) event samples that underpin critical
L000127	analyses~\cite{Buckley:2011ms,EuropeanStrategyGroup:2020pow,Narain:2022qud,HEPSoftwareFoundation:2017ggl,HSFPhysicsEventGeneratorWG:2020gxw}.
L000128	An important example is vector boson plus jets production,
L000129	for which very large samples are an essential input for many precision measurements,
L000130	e.g.\ for Higgs boson~\cite{ATLAS:2019vrd,ATLAS:2020jwz}
L000131	and top quark measurements~\cite{ATLAS:2019zrq,ATLAS:2020aln}.
L000132	These simulations require improvements in three main areas:
L000133	parametric accuracy, numerical stability, and
L000134	statistical precision.
L000135	This work addresses the third,
L000136	i.e.~how to efficiently generate large-scale MC event samples with a statistical quality that matches or exceeds that of experimental data samples.
L000137	The ATLAS Collaboration estimates that approximately 330 billion simulated events will be needed in the next phase of the LHC to accurately model vector-boson production in association with additional jets~\cite{ATLAS:2021yza}, a dominant background process in high-energy analyses.
L000138	Generating this dataset using current methods would demand
L000139	about
L000140	1000 Perlmutter 2$\times$CPU nodes for an entire year~\cite{ATLAS:2021yza}.
L000141	
L000142	The main bottleneck is evaluating matrix elements for hard-scattering processes, especially at high final-state multiplicities.
L000143	Although only computationally feasible at leading order, these processes dominate the cost due to their complexity and low unweighting efficiency $\epsilon$~\cite{Bothmann:2022thx}.
L000144	The term unweighting refers to using rejection sampling
L000145	in order to replace a weighted sample with a typically much smaller
L000146	unit-weight sample that follows the same underlying probability distribution.
L000147	This is used in high-energy physics to reduce the cost of storage
L000148	and downstream simulation steps.
L000149	The unweighting efficiency $\epsilon$
L000150	is given by the number of events in the unweighted sample
L000151	divided by the number of events in the original weighted sample.
L000152	The main event generators used by LHC experiments are based on multi-channel methods~\cite{Kleiss:1994qy} and adaptive importance sampling algorithms like \Vegas~\cite{Lepage:1977sw,Ohl:1998jn,Lepage:2020tgj}. For seven final-state particles, one typically finds $\epsilon < \SI{0.01}{\percent}$~\cite{Hoche:2019flt}.
L000153	
L000154	Modern machine learning techniques offer an alternative.
L000155	In particular, Normalizing Flows (NFs)~\cite{Tabak:2010,Tabak:2013,Dinh:2014} have been studied as drop-in replacements for \Vegas, offering more flexible function approximations~\cite{Klimek:2018mza,Bothmann:2020ywa,Gao:2020zvv,Heimel:2022wyj,Verheyen:2022tov,Heimel:2023ngj,Heimel:2024wph,Butter:2022rso}.
L000156	Improvements of up to a factor of 10 in efficiency were demonstrated for low to moderate final-state multiplicities. However, for the highest-multiplicity processes that dominate computational budgets, substantial gains have remained elusive.
L000157	
L000158	In this work, we propose---for the first time---the use
L000159	of Continuous Normalizing Flows (CNFs)~\cite{Chen:2018}
L000160	trained with the Flow Matching method~\cite{Lipman:2023,Albergo:2023building,Albergo:2023stochastic,Liu:2022}
L000161	to solve this problem.
L000162	We evaluate our approach on the two most computationally intensive processes simulated for the LHC: lepton-pair production
L000163	and top-quark pair production
L000164	with multiple jets.
L000165	We compare performance
L000166	using the unweighting efficiency $\epsilon$
L000167	as our key metric,
L000168	benchmarking against \Vegas-based methods and Normalizing Flows based on Coupling Layers~\cite{Dinh:2014,Mueller:2019,Durkan:2019}.
L000169	Our results demonstrate a significant increase in $\epsilon$: for the highest-multiplicity processes, CNFs improve $\epsilon$ by factors of up to 184, compared to the other methods.
L000170	This is enabled in part by conditioning on helicity configurations, which allows the model to learn correlations between discrete and continuous features.
L000171	
L000172	
L000173	
L000174	\textit{Phase-space sampling}---
L000175	In collider simulations, the primary quantity of interest is the scattering cross section, which measures the probability of a given scattering process to occur.
L000176	For hadronic collisions, e.g.\ at the LHC, it is given by
L000177	
L000178	\begin{equation}
L000179	  \label{eq:hadronic_cross-section1}
L000180	  \begin{split}
L000181	    \sigma_{h_1h_2\to X} &= \sum_{i,j} \int_0^1 \!\dif x_1 \int_0^1 \!\dif x_2 \  f_i(x_1,
L000182	    \mu_F)\,  f_j(x_2, \mu_F) \\
L000183	                          &\qquad\times\, \hat\sigma_{ij \to X}(x_1, x_2, \mu_R,\mu_F)\,,
L000184	  \end{split}
L000185	\end{equation}
L000186	
L000187	where the sum runs over partons in the incoming hadrons \(h_{1,2}\),
L000188	i.e.\ over quarks, antiquarks, and gluons.
L000189	The parton density functions (PDFs) \(f_{i,j}\) are usually provided as interpolation grids in \(x\) and \(\mu_F^2\)~\cite{Buckley:2014ana}.
L000190	They vary non-linearly over many orders of magnitude.
L000191	The partonic cross section \(\hat\sigma_{ij\to X}\) involves an integral over the final-state four-momenta $p_f=(E_f,\vec p_f)$
L000192	with $f=3,\ldots,m$ and $m$ being the total number of incoming and outgoing particles:
L000193	
L000194	\begin{equation}
L000195	  \label{eq:qft_cross-section1}
L000196	  \begin{split}
L000197	  \mathrm{d}\hat\sigma_{ij\to X}
L000198	  &= \frac{1}{2 E_1 E_2 \abs{\vec{v}_1-\vec{v}_2}} \, \Big( \prod_f
L000199	  \frac{\mathrm{d}^3 \vec{p}_f}{(2\pi)^3} \frac{1}{2E_f} \Big) \\
L000200	  &\quad\;\,\times \Big|\symscr{M}_{ij\to X}\big(p_1,p_2 \to \{p_f\}\big)\Big|^2 \\
L000201	  &\quad\;\,\times \, (2\pi)^4 \, \delta^{(4)}\Big(p_1+p_2-\sum_{k=3}^m p_k\Big) \Theta\left(\left\{p_f\right\}\right) \,.
L000202	  \end{split}
L000203	\end{equation}
L000204	
L000205	The cut function \(\Theta\), implemented in terms of Heaviside functions,
L000206	enforces phase-space cuts imposed by experiment or theory. The squared
L000207	matrix element \(\abs{\symscr{M}_{ij\to X}}^2\)
L000208	can be expressed via helicity amplitudes.
L000209	The Lorentz-invariant phase space includes a delta function enforcing four-momentum conservation, which reduces the dimensionality of the integral from \(3n_\text{out}\) to \(3n_\text{out} - 4\), with \(n_\text{out} = m - 2\) denoting the number of final-state particles.
L000210	Including \(x_{1,2}\), the total dimensionality becomes \(d = 3n_\text{out} - 2\), which can reach around 20 for typical LHC simulations.
L000211	
L000212	The integral \(\sigma\) in eq.~\eqref{eq:hadronic_cross-section1} defines the overall normalization and can be
L000213	approximated to the required precision by MC integration.
L000214	While the relative error of this integral estimate is also of interest,
L000215	the real challenge is to generate phase-space
L000216	samples \(x_{1,2} \cup \{\vec{p}_f\}\) distributed as \(\mathrm{d}\sigma\).
L000217	These samples must contain enough events for robust comparisons across
L000218	a large number of observables of interest,
L000219	ranging across many orders of magnitude in $\mathrm d \sigma$.
L000220	The difficulty of the task is increased
L000221	because the matrix elements \(|\symscr{M}|^2\) are usually sharply
L000222	peaked and multimodal,
L000223	and because of the discontinuities introduced by the phase-space cuts \(\Theta\).
L000224	Moreover, the integration variables are often strongly correlated.
L000225	
L000226	To simplify the notation, let \(p\) be a probability density function defined by \(p(x) \dif x = \sigma^{-1} \dif
L000227	\sigma\).
L000228	To make the problem more suitable for MC sampling,
L000229	the function \(p\) is pulled back
L000230	from the manifold of physically valid phase-space points, $M$, to the unit hypercube \(U = \intervalrightopen{0,
L000231	1}^d\) using a bijective map \(\phi: U \to M\), such that
L000232	\begin{equation}
L000233	    p_0(x) = \phi^* p(x) = p\bigl(\phi(x)\bigr) \det\biggl[{\pd{\phi}{x}}(x)\biggr] \,.
L000234	\end{equation}
L000235	The map \(\phi\) implements four-momentum conservation and Lorentz invariance. Ideally, it simplifies the problem by
L000236	using physical knowledge of the scattering process under consideration, in particular by flattening the
L000237	distribution.
L000238	Rejection sampling is used to sample from \(p\).
L000239	First, a trial event is generated by sampling from the uniform
L000240	distribution \(u_d\) on \(U\) and applying the map \(\phi\). The density of such events is
L000241	\begin{equation}
L000242	  q(x) = \phi_* u_d(x) = \det\biggl[ \pd{\phi^{-1}}{x} (x) \biggr] \,.
L000243	\end{equation}
L000244	The trial event is accepted with probability
L000245	\begin{equation}\label{eq:is_weight}
L000246	  p_{\text{accept}}(x) = \frac{w(x)}{w_\text{max}}\,, \quad \text{where} \quad w(x) \coloneq \frac{p(x)}{q(x)} \,,
L000247	\end{equation}
L000248	and rejected otherwise. Using \(w_{\text{max}} \coloneq \max_x w(x)\), the accepted events follow \(p\).
L000249	The resulting unweighting efficiency~$\epsilon$
L000250	is given by the average acceptance probability,
L000251	
L000252	\begin{equation}\label{eq:unw_eff}
L000253	  \epsilon \coloneq \frac{\langle w\rangle}{w_{\text{max}}}\,,
L000254	\end{equation}
L000255	
L000256	which is a crucial figure of merit to measure the performance of a phase-space generator.
L000257	
L000258	In practice, the maximal weight $w_{\mathrm{max}}$ needs to be estimated in an initial survey phase from a finite set of events.
L000259	To mitigate the impact of large outliers,
L000260	one usually defines an effective $w_{\mathrm{max,eff}}$ to increase the efficiency and to render its value more numerically stable.
L000261	Events with a weight $w>w_{\mathrm{max,eff}}$ are assigned a non-uniform overweight $w/w_{\mathrm{max,eff}}$.
L000262	Commonly, \(w_{\mathrm{max,eff}}\) is defined to fix the fraction of overweight events via their contribution to the integral, where e.g.\ $\epsilon_{0.001}$ denotes the unweighting efficiency for which maximally \SI{0.1}{\percent} of the integral is contributed by events with overweights.
L000263	
L000264	To increase the unweighting efficiency,
L000265	automatic optimization techniques can be an effective
L000266	alternative to manually tuning \(\phi\). To implement these,
L000267	\(\phi\) is combined
L000268	with a second bijective map,
L000269	\(\psi_{\theta}: U \to U\), a parametric model with parameters \(\theta\) that can be adapted to data.
L000270	Let $q_\theta$ be the pushforward of the uniform distribution $u_d$ by $\psi_\theta$,
L000271	\begin{equation}\label{eq:push_forward_psi}
L000272	    q_\theta = (\psi_\theta)_* u_d \,.
L000273	\end{equation}
L000274	Sampling from \(q_\theta\), the unweighting efficiency becomes
L000275	\begin{equation}\label{eq:unw_eff_theta}
L000276	  \epsilon_\theta = \frac{\langle w_\theta\rangle}{w_{\text{max},\theta}}  \quad \text{with} \quad w_\theta(x) \coloneq
L000277	    \frac{p_0(x)}{q_\theta(x)}\,.
L000278	\end{equation}
L000279	In order to maximize the unweighting efficiency, \(\psi_\theta\) needs to be optimized such that \(q_\theta\) becomes
L000280	close to \(p_0\).
L000281	
L000282	A simple example for \(\psi_\theta\) is \Vegas, which is a fully factorized approach. It constructs
L000283	\(\psi_\theta\) as a product deformation map based on one-dimensional piecewise linear maps. When \(p_0\) is not
L000284	factorizable, this approach is clearly limited. A more expressive alternative is given by NFs,
L000285	which use neural networks to parametrize \(\psi_\theta\).
L000286	Through restrictions in their architecture, NFs can be designed in a way so that their Jacobian
L000287	determinant can easily be evaluated without having to invert the neural networks. Flows based on Coupling Layers, for example,
L000288	are implemented as a chain of discrete, simpler steps.
L000289	In this work, we propose to realize \(\psi_{\theta}\) as a CNF, where the map is constructed implicitly by integrating a time-dependent vector field.
L000290	
L000291	
L000292	
L000293	\textit{Continuous Normalizing Flows}---As discussed above, we aim to sample from a target density \(p_0: \mathbb{R}^d \to
L000294	\mathbb{R}\), but only have access to a latent density \(q_0\). We seek a map \(\psi: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d\)
L000295	that transforms \(x \sim q_0\) into \(x' \sim p_0\), or approximately so. NFs provide such a map as a
L000296	trainable diffeomorphism. Different NF methods vary in construction and training objective. One example are
L000297	discrete-time flows defined as compositions \(\psi = \psi_k \circ \cdots \circ \psi_1\), often built using Coupling
L000298	Layers and trained via KL loss~\cite{10.1214/aoms/1177729694}. We instead realize \(\psi\) as
L000299	a CNF—a continuous-time analogue—which, as shown below, trains more easily and scales better with dimensionality in our application.
L000300	
L000301	A CNF is defined by a smooth, time-dependent vector field \(v_t : [0,1] \times \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d\), which
L000302	determines a flow \(\psi_t:\mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d\) via the ODE
L000303	\begin{equation}\label{eq:ode}
L000304	    \frac{\dif \psi_t(x)}{\dif t} = v_t(\psi_t(x)) \,,
L000305	\end{equation}
L000306	with initial condition \(\psi_0(x) = x\). Sampling is done by drawing \(x_0 \sim q_0\) and integrating
L000307	\begin{equation}\label{eq:cnf_sampling}
L000308	    x_t = \psi_t(x_0) = \int_0^t v_{t'}(\psi_{t'}(x_0)) \dif t' \,.
L000309	\end{equation}
L000310	If \(v_t\) is Lipschitz in space and continuous in time, the Picard--Lindelöf theorem ensures that \(\psi_t\) is bijective.
L000311	
L000312	To evaluate the density \(q_t(x_t)\),
L000313	the following continuity equation is used
L000314	\begin{equation}
L000315	    \frac{\partial}{\partial t} q_t(x) + \nabla \cdot (q_t(x) v_t(x)) = 0 \,,
L000316	\end{equation}
L000317	which leads to
L000318	\begin{equation}
L000319	    \frac{\dif}{\dif t} \log q_t(\psi_t(x_0)) + \nabla \cdot v_t(\psi_t(x_0)) = 0 \,.
L000320	\end{equation}
L000321	Thus, sampling and density evaluation reduce to solving the joint system
L000322	\begin{equation}\label{eq:joint_ode}
L000323	    \frac{\dif}{\dif t} \begin{bmatrix}
L000324	           \psi_t(x_0) \\
L000325	           \log q_t(\psi_t(x_0))
L000326	         \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
L000327	                           v_t(\psi_t(x_0)) \\
L000328	                           - \nabla \cdot v_t(\psi_t(x_0))
L000329	                          \end{bmatrix}
L000330	\end{equation}
L000331	with initial conditions
L000332	\begin{equation}
L000333	    \begin{bmatrix}
L000334	        \psi_t(x_0) \\
L000335	        \log q_t(x_0)
L000336	    \end{bmatrix}_{t=0} = \begin{bmatrix}
L000337	                        x_0 \\
L000338	                        \log q_0(x_0)
L000339	                    \end{bmatrix} \,.
L000340	\end{equation}
L000341	
L000342	Note that we define \(\psi_t\) on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) rather than the unit cube~\(U\) to avoid boundary constraints on \(v_t\),
L000343	naturally choosing a standard normal base distribution. To map outputs to \(U\),
L000344	an element-wise sigmoid transform is applied,
L000345	adjusting densities via the Jacobian determinant.
L000346	
L000347	
L000348	
L000349	\textit{Flow Matching}---We want to learn \(v_t\) (or \(\psi_t\)) so that the model density \(q_1\) matches the target \(p\). To this end, we parametrize the vector field as \(v_{t,\theta}\) via a neural network with trainable parameters \(\theta\).
L000350	Assuming access to samples \(x_1 \sim p(x)\), we aim to adapt \(v_{t,\theta}\).
L000351	One option is maximum likelihood
L000352	estimation via KL minimization
L000353	as in~\cite{Chen:2018}. However, this is slow because each training step requires integrating the reverse ODE to
L000354	evaluate the density.
L000355	
L000356	This motivates simulation-free alternatives~\cite{Lipman:2023,Liu:2022,Albergo:2023building,Albergo:2023stochastic},
L000357	which directly match \(v_{t,\theta}\) to a target vector field \(u_t\) generating \(p(x)\).
L000358	There are various ways to construct an admissible \(u_t\). We briefly outline the strategy of refs.~\cite{Lipman:2023,Tong:2024} via conditional vector fields.
L000359	Let
L000360	\begin{equation}
L000361	    u_t(x \mid x_0, x_1) = x_1 - x_0 \,,
L000362	\end{equation}
L000363	with \(x_0 \sim q_0\) and \(x_1 \sim p\) being samples from the base and target distribution, respectively.
L000364	Clearly, a particle starting at \(x_0\) will flow to \(x_1\) along a straight line when following \(u_t(\cdot \mid x_0, x_1)\), i.e.\
L000365	\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear_interp}
L000366	    x_t = t x_1 + (1-t) x_0 \,.
L000367	\end{equation}
L000368	Let now
L000369	\begin{equation}\label{eq:marginal_target_vector_field}
L000370	    u_t(x) = \mathbb{E}_{\substack{(x_0, x_1) \sim \pi,\\ x_t=x}} [u_t(x \mid x_0, x_1)] \,.
L000371	\end{equation}
L000372	It can be shown~\cite{Lipman:2023,Tong:2024,Liu:2022} that the flow according to \(u_t\) transforms \(q_0\) into \(p\) as long as the marginals of the joint law \(\pi\) are \(q_0\) and \(p\). For instance, we can set \(\pi(x_0, x_1) = q_0(x_0) \cdot p(x_1)\) (independent coupling). Note that we condition the expectation to pairs \((x_0, x_1)\) where \(x_t\), eq.~\eqref{eq:linear_interp}, moves through the query point \(x\).
L000373	It is now natural to fit \(v_{t, \theta}(x)\) to \(u_t(x)\), e.g.\ via
L000374	\begin{equation}\label{eq:FM_loss_marginal}
L000375	    \mathbb{E}_{\substack{(x_0,x_1) \sim \pi,\\ t \sim U_1}} \lVert v_{t,\theta}(x_t) - u_t(x_t) \rVert^2 \,.
L000376	\end{equation}
L000377	Note that evaluating \(u_t\) at specified points \(x\) is cumbersome due to the conditioning in
L000378	eq.~\eqref{eq:marginal_target_vector_field}. Fortunately, one can show~\cite{Lipman:2023,Tong:2024} that
L000379	eq.~\eqref{eq:FM_loss_marginal} has the same minimizers as the Flow Matching objective
L000380	\begin{equation}\label{eq:FM_loss}
L000381	    \mathcal{L}_{\text{FM}} = \mathbb{E}_{\substack{(x_0, x_1) \sim \pi,\\ t \sim U_1}} \lVert
L000382	    v_{t,\theta}(x_t) -
L000383	    u_t(x_t \mid x_0, x_1) \rVert^2 \,.
L000384	\end{equation}
L000385	It is local in space and time, so it can be evaluated fast and without solving an ODE. Also, in contrast to a KL loss,
L000386	the minimizer is unique (in terms of \(v_{t,\theta}(x)\)), which should lead to more efficient use of the model parameters.
L000387	More generally, one can add noise to the interpolation to increase
L000388	robustness~\cite{Lipman:2023,Tong:2024}. This is done by adding noise with a small
L000389	standard deviation \(\sigma_{\text{noise}}\) to the individual straight lines \(x_t\). This way, the sampled data cover
L000390	more volume. We use \(\sigma_{\text{noise}} = 10^{-4}\).
L000391	
L000392	We make two modifications to the objective. First, since the data lies in the unit hypercube \(U\),
L000393	the inverse of the sigmoid transform—namely, the logit function—is used
L000394	to map it to \(\mathbb{R}^d\). To avoid numerical issues, we combine
L000395	it with
L000396	the affine transform \(x \mapsto x \cdot (1 - \epsilon) + \epsilon/2\) with \(\epsilon = 10^{-6}\).
L000397	Second, we cannot sample \(x_1 \sim p\) directly; instead, we sample from our model distribution \(q_1 = (\psi_\theta)_*
L000398	q_0\).
L000399	To account for the mismatch in density, the loss, eq.~\ref{eq:FM_loss}, is multiplied by the importance weight \(w\),
L000400	resulting in a variant of Energy Conditional Flow Matching~\cite{Tong:2024}. Since high variance in the weights can
L000401	impair training in high dimensions, choosing a map \(\phi\) that minimizes variance is crucial.
L000402	After each training iteration, the model can generate samples to allow further iterative refinement. If available, a pre-trained \Vegas\ can also provide initial samples, enhancing the overall training process.
L000403	
L000404	\textit{Application}---We apply our neural network optimization method to dominant partonic channels in standard-candle processes at the \LHC: lepton and top pair production with additional jets at $\sqrt s = \SI{14}{\TeV}$. We focus on the channels $d \bar d \to e^+ e^- + ng$ and $g g \to t \bar t + ng$, which contribute the largest cross sections.
L000405	We use the \Chili\ phase space to define the mapping $\phi$
L000406	which maps the unit hypercube to the physical phase space~\cite{Bothmann:2023siu}.
L000407	The \Chili\ mapping is simple yet effective,
L000408	using a single integration channel that performs competitively
L000409	with complex multichannel-based methods for several standard LHC processes,
L000410	including the ones studied in this letter~\cite{Bothmann:2023gew}.
L000411	
L000412	
L000413	
L000414	To evaluate the matrix elements, we use \Pepper~\cite{Bothmann:2021nch,Bothmann:2022itv,Bothmann:2023gew},
L000415	a parton-level generator optimized for complex \LHC\ processes with GPU acceleration.
L000416	It employs an internal implementation of \Chili\ and is integrated in the LHC simulation toolchain by
L000417	interfaces to the widely used event generators \Sherpa~\cite{Gleisberg:2008ta,Sherpa:2019gpd,Sherpa:2024mfk} and
L000418	\Pythia~\cite{Bierlich:2022pfr,Sjostrand:2014zea,Sjostrand:2006za,Sjostrand:2007gs}.
L000419	Matrix elements are evaluated in \Pepper\ by summing over color indices using a minimal color decomposition~\cite{Melia:2013bta,Melia:2013xok,Johansson:2015oia}, while non-vanishing helicity configurations are sampled. Normally, the helicity sampling is adapted to minimize variance, similar to adding a discrete optimization dimension to \Vegas. Under neural network optimization, we jointly optimize phase-space and helicity sampling, exploiting correlations between them, by introducing the helicities as a conditioning variable for the networks.
L000420	For this study we have added a simple and straightforward file-based interface to \Pepper\ for training and evaluating Machine Learning models,
L000421	based on the scalable LHEH5 format~\cite{Hoeche:2019rti,Bothmann:2023ozs}.
L000422	
L000423	For the PDFs, we
L000424	use the \NNPDF3.0~\cite{NNPDF:2014otw} set via \LHAPDF6~\cite{Buckley:2014ana}. The renormalization and factorization scales are set to $\mu_R^2=\mu_F^2=H_T'^2/2$ for lepton pair production, and to $H_T^2/2$ for top-quark pair production~\cite{Bern:2013gka}.
L000425	The following electroweak parameters are used:
L000426	$\sin^2\theta_w=0.23155$, $\alpha=1/128.80$, $m_Z=91.1876$\,GeV, $\Gamma_Z=2.4952$\,GeV, and the top-quark mass $m_t=173.21$\,GeV. All other quarks are massless.
L000427	For lepton pairs we additionally require $66\,\mathrm{GeV} \leq m_{e^+e^-} \leq 116\,\mathrm{GeV}$.
L000428	For all massless partons we enforce the additional jet cuts $p_{T,j}>30\,\mathrm{GeV}$, $|\eta_j|<5$, and $\Delta R_{ij}>0.4$.
L000429	
L000430	All \Vegas\ grids have 100 bins per dimension, optimized over 15 steps. For the highest multiplicity, we use $\sim$$3\cdot10^8$ points, yielding $2.5\cdot10^5$ training points per non-vanishing helicity configuration in lepton-pair production and $8\cdot10^4$ for top-quark pair production.
L000431	The NFs are optimized in 8 iterations.
L000432	Initially, events are generated without remapping.
L000433	In subsequent steps, trained flows produce inputs used by \Pepper\ to generate training data.
L000434	An embedding layer encodes helicity configurations.
L000435	Our Coupling Flows use the minimal number of layers needed to capture correlations,
L000436	with transformations based on multilayer perceptrons (MLPs),
L000437	and an early stopping criterion based on validation loss.
L000438	Our ODE Flows use MLP-based vector fields with time encoded via Fourier features~\cite{Tancik:2020}.
L000439	Both models are trained using the AdamW optimizer~\cite{adamw:2019}.
L000440	After training, the models are frozen and used to generate weighted events.
