L000456	\textit{Results}---We report relative improvements in the unweighting efficiency $\epsilon_{0.001}$, which serves as the primary performance metric of this study.
L000457	Figure~\ref{fig:unweff} illustrates the results for the processes $d\bar d \to e^+e^-+ng$ and $gg \to t\bar t + ng$,
L000458	comparing the Coupling Flow and the ODE Flow mappings to \textsc{Vegas}.
L000459	We observe that ODE Flows exhibit a favourable scaling of $\epsilon_{0.001}$ with increasing $n$, outperforming the other methods as the complexity grows.
L000460	In contrast, the performance of Coupling Flows deteriorates at the highest multiplicity studied, with efficiency falling below that of the \textsc{Vegas} baseline in the $gg \to t\bar t + 4g$ case.
L000461	For $d\bar d \to e^+e^-+5g$, the ODE Flow achieves $\epsilon_{0.001} = \SI{1.29(8)}{\percent}$, which is about 43$\times$ higher than the result for the Coupling Flow and 184$\times$ higher than the one for \Vegas.
L000462	For $\epsilon_{0.01}$, the relative gains are 13 and 153, respectively.
L000463	For $gg \to t\bar t + 4g$, the ODE Flow yields $\epsilon_{0.001} = \SI{5.76(9)}{\percent}$, outperforming the Coupling Flow by a factor of 144 and \Vegas\ by a factor of 25.
L000464	For $\epsilon_{0.01}$, the respective relative gains are 8 and 17.
L000465	
L000466	Unweighting efficiency improvements of the ODE Flow over \Vegas\ are consistently greater for $d\bar d \to e^+e^-+ng$ than for $gg \to t\bar t + ng$. At $n=4$, we see a factor 198 improvement of $\epsilon_{0.001}$ for the former, versus a factor 25 for the latter. This is due to a better \Vegas\ baseline performance for top-pair production, visible also in the relative integration errors we find. This suggests structural differences in phase-space factorization---e.g.\ due to the $Z$-boson resonance and the stronger dependence on the helicity configuration in the lepton-pair production case.
L000467	
L000468	Our findings demonstrate that Flow Matching outperforms alternative approaches, in particular in terms of the unweighting efficiency---the key metric for efficient event generation. Its advantage over Coupling Flows is largest at high multiplicities, where computational cost is highest, making it a highly promising candidate for current and future LHC simulation campaigns.
L000469	
L000470	Although the ODE-based models incur longer inference times, their
L000471	performance can be efficiently transferred to fast Coupling Flows using
L000472	the recently proposed RegFlow method~\cite{Rehman:2025}, which trains the
L000473	discrete model on pairs generated by the ODE Flow. In our benchmarks,
L000474	RegFlow-trained Coupling Flows recover a sizable fraction of the ODE
L000475	efficiencies while maintaining two orders of magnitude faster inference,
L000476	yielding effective walltime gains of a factor of 12 for $d \bar d \to e^+ e^-\,5g$
L000477	and 8 for $gg \to t \bar t\,4g$ when compared to \Vegas.
L000478	The longer training is offset by the speed-up
L000479	after two million events for $d \bar d \to e^+ e^-\,5g$,
L000480	and after forty million events for $gg \to t \bar t\,4g$.
